This Python project generates a PDF report showing weekly average and weekly sum visualizations for key player performance metrics. It’s a dual-panel comparison of how players perform on average and in total each week — with statistical context like standard deviation and mean lines.

Input:

player_profiles.csv: A CSV file containing player tracking data across multiple weeks.
Required Columns:
Week
Player Name
Player Load
Sprint Distance
Top Speed (only used in the average plots)
Power Plays
Distance

Output

player_stats_all_weeks_combined.pdf: A multi-page PDF with one page per week, containing:

Top Row: Weekly averages (with overall mean ± standard deviation)

Bottom Row: Weekly sums (with week-level mean ± standard deviation)

Each row includes the following metric visualizations:
Player Load
Sprint Distance
Power Plays
Distance
(Top Speed is included in averages only)


Visualization Details

Top Row: Weekly Averages
Plots per player per metric
Horizontal lines:
Solid Black: Global average across all weeks and players
Dashed Black: ±1 global standard deviation
Custom y-axis:
Top Speed: [10, 24]
Others: up to 1.5× weekly max
Bottom Row: Weekly Sums
Plots per player per metric
Horizontal lines:
Solid Black: Week's average
Dashed Black: ±1 standard deviation for the current week
Custom y-axis:
Power Plays: up to 3.5× max
Others: up to 3× max


What It Does:
- Loads player data
- Computes weekly averages and sums per player
- Calculates overall and per-week mean/std dev values
- Generates bar plots comparing players’ performance
- Exports to PDF for easy sharing or printing
